 "questions": {
  "q1_uniform_generation": "The previous task generated departures with depart_times(): a fixed interval 3600/flow placed from phase*interval, rounded up to 0.1 s. It is a deterministic periodic schedule, so it carries no arrival clustering.",
  "q2_network_entry_position": "Netconvert extends main_0 to the junction midpoint; parse_fcd inverse-scales built positions back onto the observed span, so built position 0 corresponds to real y = 0 m. departPos='base' therefore enters a vehicle at real y of a few metres.",
  "q3_flow_plane_vs_entry": "They did NOT coincide before this task: the demand was derived from trajectory crossings of y = 150 m and applied at a network entry near y = 0 m, a 150 m mismatch. This task replaces that with the observed first-appearance events, whose median first position is ~11 m (0750) and ~23 m (0805), i.e. the reliably covered upstream strip, and it declares ENTRY_Y_MAX_M = 30 m.",
  "q4_available_boundary_fields": "Per event the canonical states give the crossing/first-observation time, lane, position and speed, and the off-ramp membership (a trajectory ever observed on lane 8). Vehicle type is NOT available per vehicle: the canonical schema has no per-vehicle length or class, so the previous task's aggregate fleet shares and its deterministic class assignment are inherited unchanged and are used identically in both arms.",
  "q5_initial_state_double_counting": "Vehicles whose first observation is exactly at the window start are the injected initial state; they are excluded from the entry sequence by construction. The audit reports the intersection explicitly for each window.",
  "q6_reintroduction_of_dropped_vehicles": "Vehicles dropped by the initial-state overlap filter have their first observation at the window start, so they are excluded from the entry sequence as well: they are simply absent, never re-introduced. Vehicles first observed downstream of the entry plane (occluded at the boundary) are excluded and recorded."
 },
